-- Q1: hard / L3 — CUECs and their impact on control risk assessment
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$Harmon Industries relies on PayStream Corp. for payroll processing. The SOC 1 Type 2 report for PayStream lists three complementary user entity controls (CUECs), including reconciliation of payroll registers to the general ledger. The user auditor discovers that Harmon has not implemented any of the listed CUECs. The user auditor should determine that the most appropriate response is to:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Assess control risk as higher for payroll assertions and expand substantive testing", "Issue a qualified opinion for the scope limitation caused by PayStream", "Request a new SOC report that excludes CUECs from the scope", "Conclude that PayStream's controls alone provide sufficient assurance"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): AU-C 402.15. CUECs are controls the service organization's system design assumes the user entity will implement. When the user entity has not implemented required CUECs, the combined control environment has gaps, and the user auditor must assess control risk as higher and design additional substantive procedures to address the increased risk.
Wrong (B): Failure to implement CUECs is not a scope limitation — the auditor can still perform alternative procedures.
Wrong (C): CUECs are inherent to the service organization's control design; removing them from the report is not appropriate.
Wrong (D): The SOC report explicitly contemplates that CUECs must be operating at the user entity for the controls to function as designed.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Restricted use of SOC 1 reports
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$NorthStar Analytics obtains a SOC 1 Type 2 report from its data processing vendor. NorthStar's CFO wants to share the report with a prospective investor to demonstrate strong vendor controls. When advising the CFO on the distribution of this report, the user auditor should explain that a SOC 1 report is:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Available for general public distribution without restriction", "Restricted to the user entities and their auditors", "Restricted solely to management of the service organization", "Available to any party that signs a confidentiality agreement"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AT-C 320.53. SOC 1 reports are restricted-use reports intended only for management of the service organization, user entities, and user auditors. They are not intended for general distribution or for parties who do not have sufficient knowledge to understand the report.
Wrong (A): SOC 1 reports are restricted-use, not general-use reports. SOC 3 reports are the general-use alternative.
Wrong (C): The reports are also available to user entities and their auditors, not solely service organization management.
Wrong (D): A confidentiality agreement does not expand the intended audience of a restricted-use report.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Internal audit charter role in evaluation
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$During the 2025 audit of Benson Holdings, the external auditor requests to review the internal audit department's charter as part of evaluating the internal audit function. The primary reason the external auditor reviews the internal audit charter is to:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Determine the internal auditors' individual educational qualifications", "Verify the budgeted hours for internal audit activities", "Assess the organizational status and authority granted to the internal audit function", "Confirm that internal auditors hold professional certifications"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): AU-C 610.A15. The internal audit charter defines the function's purpose, authority, and reporting relationships within the organization. Reviewing the charter helps the external auditor evaluate the organizational status of the internal audit function, which directly affects its ability to perform work objectively and with appropriate authority.
Wrong (A): Individual educational qualifications are evaluated separately and are not the primary purpose of reviewing the charter.
Wrong (B): Budgeted hours relate to resource allocation, not the authority and status that the charter establishes.
Wrong (D): Professional certifications are relevant to competence but are assessed through other means, not the charter.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Definition of SOC 3 report
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$What type of SOC report is designed for general use and provides a seal or short-form report on whether a service organization's controls meet the trust services criteria?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["SOC 1 Type 1", "SOC 1 Type 2", "SOC 2 Type 2", "SOC 3"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): AT-C 205. A SOC 3 report is a general-use report that provides a short-form opinion on whether controls at a service organization meet the trust services criteria. Unlike SOC 1 and SOC 2 reports, SOC 3 reports do not contain detailed descriptions of tests and results and can be freely distributed.
Wrong (A): SOC 1 Type 1 reports address controls relevant to user entities' financial reporting and are restricted-use.
Wrong (B): SOC 1 Type 2 reports are also restricted-use and focus on financial reporting controls.
Wrong (C): SOC 2 Type 2 reports address trust services criteria but are restricted-use with detailed testing results.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: easy / L2 — Agreed-upon procedures when SOC report unavailable
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$Kellerman Manufacturing uses a small payroll bureau that does not have a SOC report. The user auditor needs to evaluate the payroll bureau's controls. An appropriate alternative for the user auditor is to:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Perform tests of controls at the service organization or arrange for agreed-upon procedures to be performed", "Automatically issue a scope limitation in the auditor's report", "Accept management's written assertion that the bureau's controls are adequate", "Treat all payroll transactions as immaterial to avoid the issue"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): AU-C 402.12-.13. When a SOC report is not available, the user auditor may perform procedures at the service organization, use another auditor to perform procedures, or obtain an agreed-upon procedures report from a practitioner at the service organization. These alternatives allow the auditor to obtain sufficient evidence about the service organization's controls.
Wrong (B): A scope limitation is not automatic — the auditor has several alternative procedures available before concluding on a scope limitation.
Wrong (C): Management's assertion alone does not constitute sufficient appropriate audit evidence about controls.
Wrong (D): Payroll transactions cannot simply be dismissed as immaterial without a proper materiality assessment.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Component auditor communication to group team
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$In a group audit, what is the component auditor required to communicate to the group engagement team?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Only the final component auditor's opinion", "Matters relevant to the group engagement team's conclusion, including significant risks and deficiencies", "Only misstatements that exceed group materiality", "A complete copy of all working papers without summarization"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 600.42-.44. The component auditor must communicate matters relevant to the group engagement team's conclusion, including identified significant risks of material misstatement, significant control deficiencies, fraud or suspected fraud, and other matters that may be relevant to the group audit.
Wrong (A): The component auditor must communicate more than just the final opinion — specific findings and risks must be reported.
Wrong (C): Communication is not limited to misstatements exceeding group materiality; significant risks and deficiencies must also be communicated.
Wrong (D): Complete working paper copies are not required to be transmitted; relevant matters and summaries are communicated.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Direct assistance written agreement requirements
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$Thornton CPA firm plans to use two internal auditors from Lakeshore Corp. to provide direct assistance on inventory observations at 12 locations. Before the internal auditors begin any work, Thornton obtains a written agreement. The written agreement for direct assistance should specifically address the internal auditors' obligation to:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Report directly to Lakeshore's audit committee during the engagement", "Independently design their own testing procedures based on internal audit methodology", "Keep confidential the matters related to the audit and follow the external auditor's direction", "Communicate findings only through Lakeshore's chief audit executive"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): AU-C 610.28-.29. The written agreement for direct assistance must address confidentiality of audit matters and the internal auditors' obligation to follow the external auditor's direction and instructions. This ensures the external auditor maintains control over the work performed and that sensitive audit information is protected.
Wrong (A): During direct assistance, internal auditors work under the external auditor's direction, not the audit committee's.
Wrong (B): Direct assistance requires the internal auditors to follow the external auditor's procedures, not independently design their own.
Wrong (D): Findings should be communicated directly to the external auditor, not filtered through the chief audit executive.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Service organization management assertion
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$In a SOC 1 engagement, the service organization's management provides a written assertion. What does this management assertion primarily address?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The profitability and financial viability of the service organization", "The fairness of the service organization's own financial statements", "The description of the system and the suitability of design and operating effectiveness of controls", "The user entities' satisfaction with the services provided"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): AT-C 320.12-.13. In a SOC 1 engagement, management's written assertion addresses whether the description of the service organization's system is fairly presented, whether the controls were suitably designed, and (for Type 2) whether the controls operated effectively throughout the specified period.
Wrong (A): The management assertion is about the system description and control design/effectiveness, not the organization's profitability.
Wrong (B): A SOC 1 engagement is not an audit of the service organization's financial statements — it examines controls relevant to user entities' financial reporting.
Wrong (D): User entity satisfaction is not part of the management assertion in a SOC engagement.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Internal audit function restricted to lower-risk areas
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$The external auditor of Riverdale Corp. determines that the internal audit function is competent and objective. The external auditor plans to use internal audit work across multiple areas. When deciding which audit areas are appropriate for using internal auditors' work, the external auditor should assign the internal audit function to areas that involve:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Lower assessed risk of material misstatement and less subjective judgment", "The highest risk accounts to maximize audit efficiency", "Only financial statement areas where controls have already been tested", "Any area regardless of risk level since objectivity has been established"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): AU-C 610.21-.23. Even when the internal audit function is competent and objective, the external auditor should use their work primarily in areas of lower assessed risk that involve less judgment. Areas of higher risk, significant estimates, and significant judgments require more direct involvement from the external auditor.
Wrong (B): Higher risk areas require the external auditor's direct involvement, not delegation to internal auditors.
Wrong (C): Internal auditors' work is not limited to areas where controls have been tested — they may assist with substantive procedures in appropriate areas.
Wrong (D): Established objectivity does not eliminate the restriction on using internal audit work in higher-risk, more judgmental areas.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Group audit documentation requirements and judgment
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$Grant & Ellis LLP serves as the group engagement team for Worldwide Corp., which has 14 components across eight countries. The group engagement partner must determine what to include in the group audit documentation. During the quality review, a reviewer questions whether the documentation is sufficient. The group engagement team's audit documentation should include all of the following EXCEPT:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The analysis used to identify significant components", "Copies of all component auditors' complete working paper files", "The nature, timing, and extent of the group engagement team's involvement in component auditors' work", "Communications with component auditors about independence and competence requirements"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 600.50-.51. The group engagement team must document the analysis of components, instructions to component auditors, communications about independence and ethics, and the nature and extent of involvement in component work. However, the group team is not required to obtain or maintain copies of all component auditors' complete working paper files — summaries and relevant findings are sufficient.
Wrong (A): The analysis identifying significant components is a required element of group audit documentation.
Wrong (C): Documentation of the group team's involvement in component work is explicitly required.
Wrong (D): Communications regarding independence and competence requirements must be documented.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: hard / L4 — Evaluating conflicting CUECs and subservice controls
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$Meridian Financial uses TechPay for payroll processing. TechPay's SOC 1 report uses the carve-out method for its data hosting subservice organization, CloudNest. The report lists CUECs requiring Meridian to reconcile payroll output, and separately notes that CloudNest's controls over data integrity are excluded from the report scope. The user auditor for Meridian discovers that CloudNest recently experienced a data breach affecting payroll records. The user auditor's most appropriate response is to:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Accept the SOC 1 report as sufficient since TechPay's controls were tested", "Obtain information about CloudNest's controls and evaluate the impact of the breach on payroll data integrity", "Disclaim an opinion because the carve-out method creates an inherent scope limitation", "Request TechPay to reissue the report using the inclusive method"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 402.14-.16. When the carve-out method is used and the subservice organization's controls are relevant to the user entity's financial statements, the user auditor must consider whether additional procedures are needed regarding the subservice organization. Given the data breach at CloudNest, the auditor must obtain evidence about the impact on data integrity rather than relying solely on TechPay's carved-out report.
Wrong (A): A carve-out report explicitly excludes the subservice organization's controls, so the SOC 1 alone is insufficient when those controls are compromised.
Wrong (C): The carve-out method does not create an inherent scope limitation — the auditor has alternative procedures available.
Wrong (D): Requesting a reissued report is impractical and does not address the immediate need to evaluate the breach's impact.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Definition of direct assistance
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$What is meant by "direct assistance" in the context of using internal auditors during an external audit?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Internal auditors independently perform their own audit plan alongside the external audit", "Internal auditors provide consulting advice on industry matters to the external auditor", "Internal auditors perform audit procedures under the direction, supervision, and review of the external auditor", "Internal auditors review the external auditor's working papers for completeness"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): AU-C 610.03. Direct assistance means the external auditor uses internal auditors to perform audit procedures under the external auditor's direction, supervision, and review. The external auditor retains responsibility for the work performed and must evaluate the internal auditors' competence and objectivity before using them in this capacity.
Wrong (A): Direct assistance requires internal auditors to work under the external auditor's direction, not independently on their own plan.
Wrong (B): Providing consulting advice is not direct assistance — it involves performing specific audit procedures under external auditor supervision.
Wrong (D): Reviewing the external auditor's working papers is not a form of direct assistance from internal auditors.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Roll-forward procedures for SOC report gap
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$Beacon Corp.'s fiscal year ends December 31, 2025. The user auditor obtains a SOC 1 Type 2 report from Beacon's claims processor covering January 1 through September 30, 2025. To address the October through December gap period, the user auditor considers performing roll-forward procedures. Appropriate roll-forward procedures include:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Inquiring of the service organization about significant changes to controls and reviewing a bridge letter", "Requesting a new SOC 1 engagement covering only the gap period", "Accepting management's oral representation that no changes occurred", "Extrapolating the SOC 1 results to cover the full year without additional procedures"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): AU-C 402.17-.18. When the SOC report does not cover the full period, the user auditor should perform procedures to address the remaining period, including inquiring about significant changes to controls during the gap period, reviewing a bridge letter from the service organization, and performing additional substantive procedures as needed.
Wrong (B): Requesting a separate SOC engagement solely for the gap period is impractical and not a standard roll-forward procedure.
Wrong (C): Oral representations alone are insufficient — the auditor needs corroborating evidence such as a bridge letter or inquiry responses.
Wrong (D): Extrapolating SOC results without additional procedures does not provide sufficient evidence for the uncovered period.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Scope limitation when component restricts group team involvement
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$During the group audit of Cascade International, the group engagement team requests to review the working papers and participate in the planning of a significant component audited by Werner & Co. Werner & Co. cooperates on all communications but states that firm policy prohibits external review of their detailed working papers. The group engagement partner should evaluate this situation and determine that the most appropriate action is to:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Accept the restriction without further action since Werner & Co. communicates findings", "Evaluate whether alternative procedures — such as reviewing summary memoranda, discussions, and additional group-level testing — can provide sufficient evidence", "Immediately replace Werner & Co. with a different component auditor", "Issue an adverse opinion on the consolidated financial statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 600.41 and .A56-.A57. When access to component auditor working papers is restricted, the group engagement partner should evaluate whether sufficient appropriate audit evidence can be obtained through alternative means, such as reviewing summary memoranda, holding discussions with the component auditor, and performing additional procedures at the group level. The goal is to determine whether the restriction can be overcome.
Wrong (A): The group engagement partner cannot simply accept the restriction — a positive determination that sufficient evidence is obtainable is required.
Wrong (C): Immediately replacing the component auditor may not be practical or necessary if alternative procedures can provide sufficient evidence.
Wrong (D): An adverse opinion addresses material misstatements in the financial statements, not access restrictions on working papers.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Internal audit systematic and disciplined approach
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$During the 2025 audit of Oakridge Manufacturing, the external auditor evaluates the internal audit function and finds that internal auditors have strong technical skills and report directly to the audit committee. However, the external auditor notes that the internal audit function does not follow established professional standards, has no formal quality assurance program, and does not document its methodology. The external auditor should conclude that the most significant concern about using this function's work is the lack of:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Direct reporting to the audit committee", "A sufficient number of internal audit staff members", "A systematic and disciplined approach including quality practices", "Relevant industry experience among internal auditors"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): AU-C 610.A13-.A14. When evaluating whether to use the internal audit function's work, the external auditor considers whether the function applies a systematic and disciplined approach, including quality control. Without adherence to professional standards and a quality assurance program, the reliability of the internal auditors' work is diminished regardless of individual competence or reporting structure.
Wrong (A): The function already reports to the audit committee, which supports objectivity — this is not the concern identified.
Wrong (B): Staffing levels are not identified as the primary concern; the issue is methodology and quality practices.
Wrong (D): Industry experience is relevant to competence but is secondary to the fundamental concern about systematic methodology and quality assurance.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
